  <!-- NEW SECTION: RADIATOR SIZING -->
  <section class="pf-section" style="background:#f8fafc;">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="pf-section-label">RADIATOR SIZING</span>
      <h2>Increasing or Decreasing Radiator Size in Liverpool</h2>
      <p class="pf-sub">Got a room that never warms up? Or one that runs too hot? The rad is often the wrong size for the room. We can swap it for the right one.</p>

      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:1.5rem;margin-top:2rem;">

        <div class="pf-card pf-card-left-orange">
          <strong style="display:block;color:var(--navy);font-size:1rem;margin-bottom:0.35rem;">Undersized Radiator: Room Will Not Warm Up</strong>
          <p style="font-size:0.93rem;color:var(--muted);margin:0;line-height:1.65;">If your rad is too small for the room, the heat output is too low. The room never gets warm even when the boiler is at full tilt. A bigger rad or a double panel swap fixes this. We work out the BTU output the room needs first. Then we pick a rad that hits that number.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="pf-card pf-card-left-red">
          <strong style="display:block;color:var(--navy);font-size:1rem;margin-bottom:0.35rem;">Oversized Radiator: Room Runs Too Hot</strong>
          <p style="font-size:0.93rem;color:var(--muted);margin:0;line-height:1.65;">If your rad is too big for the room, the room gets too hot too fast. The boiler short-cycles and gas use goes up. A smaller rad or a TRV fitted on the current one keeps things in check. A right-sized rad is more energy efficient. You burn less gas for the same warm room.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="pf-card pf-card-left-orange">
          <strong style="display:block;color:var(--navy);font-size:1rem;margin-bottom:0.35rem;">Why We See Wrong-Sized Radiators in Liverpool Homes</strong>
          <p style="font-size:0.93rem;color:var(--muted);margin:0;line-height:1.65;">Common reasons we get called for sizing changes: a new room layout. A new extension. A loft conversion. Or just a rad that was wrong from day one. We size each rad based on room size, wall type, window size and floor type. We give you the right output, every time.</p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="pf-alert" style="margin-top:2rem;">
        <strong>How rad sizing works</strong>
        <p>Every room needs a set BTU output to stay warm. The number depends on the room size, the insulation level, the number of windows and what is above and below the room. A rad with too low an output will leave the room cold. A rad with too high an output wastes gas. We work out the correct BTU at the quote visit and pick a rad that hits the number. Sizing changes start at £150 per rad.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

