  <!-- SIGNS RADIATORS NEED ATTENTION -->
  <section class="pf-section" style="background:#f8fafc;">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Signs Your Liverpool Radiators Need Attention</h2>
      <p class="pf-sub">These are the most common rad problems we attend across Liverpool. Most are quick to diagnose and fix.</p>

      <div class="pf-signs" style="margin-top:2rem;">
        <div class="pf-sign"><div><strong>Cold at the bottom, warm at the top</strong><p>Sludge and magnetite settle at the bottom of the rad and block the heat. Bleeding will not fix it. A power flush is the right step. If more than one rad in your Liverpool home has a cold bottom, the whole system needs a flush.</p></div></div>
        <div class="pf-sign"><div><strong>Cold at the top, warm at the bottom</strong><p>Air in the system rises to the top of the rad and pushes water down. Bleeding with a rad key fixes this. If the rad keeps needing a bleed, air is getting in. The cause is often a worn pump seal or a small leak in the circuit. Worth a check.</p></div></div>
        <div class="pf-sign"><div><strong>Radiator completely cold</strong><p>A fully cold rad on a working system often means a stuck TRV. Or a block in the rad itself. We can find this on a visit and often fix it on the same call. No need to swap the whole rad in most cases.</p></div></div>
        <div class="pf-sign"><div><strong>Radiator leaking at valve or connection</strong><p>A small drip from a valve or pipe can often be fixed by tightening the gland nut. Or swapping the valve insert. If left, a leaking rad can damage the floor and ceiling below. Worse in Liverpool's multi-floor homes.</p></div></div>
        <div class="pf-sign"><div><strong>Radiator making noise</strong><p>A tick when the heating comes on is normal. The rad is expanding. A bang, gurgle or whistle that keeps going means air in the system. Or sludge moving around. Or pump trouble. We find the cause before we suggest any work.</p></div></div>
        <div class="pf-sign"><div><strong>Radiator visibly corroded or damaged</strong><p>Surface rust on an old rad speeds up rust inside. A rad with rust through the panel or any physical damage needs a swap, not a fix. A rusting rad also sheds iron oxide into the system, which builds up sludge across the whole circuit.</p></div></div>
      </div>

      <div style="background:#f97316;border-radius:10px;padding:1.5rem;text-align:center;margin-top:2rem;">
        <p style="color:#ffffff;font-weight:700;font-size:1rem;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Not sure what is wrong with your rad? Call us and we can usually work it out over the phone before booking a visit.</p>
